// An explicitly instantiated generic call resolves its **callee** under a `namespace`, exactly as
// the plain call does.
//
// Regression: the linker's qualification walk recursed into `Expr::TypedCall`'s type arguments and
// its arguments but never visited the callee NAME, which that node holds inline rather than as an
// `Expr::Ident` sub-expression (the plain `f(args)` form reaches the rewrite through `Expr::Call`'s
// callee). So `gen(1)` resolved and `gen::<Todo>(2)` was an E0005 in the very same module — every
// generic function unusable with an explicit turbofish in any namespaced module.
//
// The three sibling turbofish surfaces are pinned alongside, because they must keep working and are
// qualified by different routes: a method/associated call resolves its name against the receiver
// (never namespaced) and reaches its receiver as a real sub-expression, and a typed module call
// reaches its module handle the same way. Only the bare-callee form needed the explicit visit.
//
// A `main.noe` on purpose: the harness runs a *single-file* case straight from source text, with no
// loader and therefore no namespace qualification at all, so a bare `.noe` here could not observe
// the bug it pins.
// Its `noeta.toml` is what makes the case a package, and therefore what derives the entry's
// module path: delete the manifest and nothing qualifies, silently.
// expect: stdout "plain: 1"
// expect: stdout "turbofish: 2"
// expect: stdout "through a helper: 7"
// expect: stdout "associated: 3"
// expect: stdout "method: 4"
// expect: stdout "module: 7"
// expect: exit 0

use std.json;

pub struct Todo { pub id: int }

fn gen<T>(x: int): int { return x }

// The callee is also reached from *inside* another declaration, which is the shape the linker's
// same-module reference graph walks: `helper` must drag `gen` into the merged program.
fn helper(): int { return gen::<Todo>(7) }

trait Marker { fn tag(): string }

pub struct Box2 { pub id: int }
impl Marker for Box2 {
    fn tag(): string { return "t" }
    fn pick<T>(x: int): int { return x }
}

echo "plain: ${gen(1)}"
echo "turbofish: ${gen::<Todo>(2)}"
echo "through a helper: ${helper()}"
echo "associated: ${Box2.pick::<Todo>(3)}"
echo "method: ${Box2 { id: 1 }.pick::<Todo>(4)}"
echo "module: ${json.parse::<Todo>("{\"id\": 7}").id}"
